## The Lab Rule of 7

The Lab Rule of 7 describes the price multiple at 3ct: a 3ct lab radiant ring costs 7× less than the 3ct natural entry.

D-VVS1 IGI lab ring: [$6,210](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28929424?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). G-VS2 GIA natural entry ring: [$42,812](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28034285?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). G-VS1 GIA natural sweet spot ring: [$45,854](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29205249?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

The lab is not a cheaper version of the same thing. It is the same thing — molecularly, visually, dimensionally identical — for a fraction of the price.

At 1ct, the lab advantage was meaningful. At 2ct, it was compelling. At 3ct, the Lab Rule of 7 makes the financial case so extreme that natural buyers need to have a reason that goes beyond budget — they need a conviction about origin.

The [lab grown radiant cut diamond](/lab-grown-radiant-cut-diamond) guide covers the certification and chemistry in detail. The short version: IGI certifies lab stones at 3ct with the same rigor as GIA certifies natural stones. A D-VVS1 IGI lab radiant is a genuine stone with a genuine certificate.

At [$6,210](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28929424?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) complete for a 9.2mm face-up stone, the 3ct lab radiant is the best face-up per dollar in the entire radiant weight series. No weight delivers more visual presence for the money at this size.

